children.translated_text:
You have prevailed, O much-invoked one, over your enemies — may your greatest fury and generosity be here!
O <s>Indra</s>, bring treasures with your right hand; you are the lord of bountiful rivers, <s>svāhā</s>!
May <s>Indra</s> [be] on our side in battles, at our standards; may our arrows be victorious;
may our heroes prevail; may the <s>devas</s> (gods) favor us in our invocations, <s>svāhā</s>!
<s>Indra</s> became the king of this universe; <s>Indra</s> upheld the earth and also this heaven;
<s>Indra</s> indeed [rules over] all beings and worlds that depend [on him], <s>svāhā</s>!
<s>Indra</s> is heaven, the firmament, the earth; <s>Indra</s> is the origin, the sovereign <s>Virāṭ</s> [who pervades] after;
the vast atmosphere and the heavenly wind — I consider <s>Indra</s> as father and mother, <s>svāhā</s>!
<s>Indra</s>, the one who leads forth, the radiant form; <s>Indra</s>, the flowing one — may <s>Savitṛ</s> establish <s>Indra</s>;
may <s>Śakra</s> [grant] the world of the meritorious — I consider <s>Indra</s> as father and mother, <s>svāhā</s>!

The possessor of splendid might upheld all, with the three sheaths, with the ten [vital airs] that were in the beginning;
for these born ones, with invocation, I worship, <s>svāhā</s>!
The waters sustain all vital breaths; he placed [them] below heaven and moving through space;
may the four-horned one come as the invoker, <s>svāhā</s>!
Dexterity they place in this [being], those offspring;
of the four-fold qualities, from the waters is this, <s>svāhā</s>!
Through her the <s>Ādityas</s> enter all beings, she who carries the oblations of all qualities,
to her clad in the best garments, may the well-offered oblation be placed, <s>svāhā</s>!
Those three she sustains, all that is here below; to whom the sages come, journeying to heaven,
in her fire the [offerings] are raised up — I offer oblation, <s>svāhā</s>!

He who is indeed the union, delighting in joining, the junction-point of those who kindle,
establishing the union, the meritorious among the meritorious — to merit, <s>svāhā</s>!
A thousand indeed is his canopy, establishing a thousand;
the blessings, the givers of blessings by the thousand — whose blessings indeed number a thousand — to the thousandfold, <s>svāhā</s>!
The protections that go beyond the Self, protection, truth — the truth of truths, the essence, the state;
that essence was essence; essence indeed — to the one who establishes essence, <s>svāhā</s>!
Essence indeed was the surplus; what is essence is the protector of both;
that essence, truth — to the speaker of truth, to truth, <s>svāhā</s>!
Truth is light, the inner Self of truth, the exertion of truth, truth is righteous action;
truth was the canopy of truth — to the truth of truths, <s>svāhā</s>!

He who is the beginning of the <s>Vedas</s>, the supreme, the transcendent <s>Brahman</s>, of three <s>mātrās</s> (measures), of triple form,
the Word and that which transcends the word — to the supreme <s>Brahman</s>, to the supreme Light, <s>svāhā</s>!
He who indeed is most desirous of cows (light);
homage to the supreme, to the Self of <s>Parameṣṭhin</s> (the Supreme Being), to <s>Viṣṇu</s>, to <s>Ākāśa</s> (Space)!
He who indeed is <s>Vāyu</s> (Wind);
homage to the supreme, to the Self that is <s>Puruṣa</s> (the Cosmic Person), to <s>Puruṣa</s>, to <s>Vāyu</s>!
You, O <s>Agni</s>, of three <s>guṇas</s> (qualities), the most excellent;
homage to the supreme, to the Self of all, to <s>Satya</s> (Truth), to <s>Agni</s>!
You are the living being, you are the waters;
homage to the supreme, to the Self of cessation, to <s>Acyuta</s> (the Imperishable), to the waters!

The one syllable [<s>Oṃ</s>];
all are submerged [in him];
<s>prāṇa</s> (breath) is the impeller;
having stretched the arrow;
you are the bearer of the <s>vajra</s> (thunderbolt);
<s>Dhātṛ</s> (Creator) and <s>Vidhātṛ</s> (Ordainer);
the <s>Ṛc</s> verses and the <s>Yajus</s> formulas;
he is that <s>deva</s> (god);
he is the knower of all;
with the <s>Sāmans</s> and with their limbs;
you are woman, you are man;
<s>Mitra</s> (the Friend), <s>Suparṇa</s> (the Beautiful-winged One);
you are <s>Viṣṇu</s>, [you are] all beings;
you are <s>bhūḥ</s> (earth), <s>bhuvaḥ</s> (atmosphere), you;
he who thus eternally [knows];
the supreme goal of the wise.
<s>Oṃ</s>! Homage to the Bow (<s>Dhanus</s>)!
To <s>Śakti</s> (the Spear), <s>svāhā</s>!
To the Mace (<s>Gadā</s>), <s>vaṣaṭ</s>!
To the Sword (<s>Khaḍga</s>), <s>hum</s>!
To the Discus (<s>Cakra</s>), <s>vauṣaṭ</s>!
To <s>Vāsuki</s> (the Serpent), <s>phaṭ</s>!
May <s>Viṣṇu</s> protect me!
Homage to <s>Keśava</s>, the bearer of the discus, whose radiance is like <s>jāmbūnada</s> (gold)!
<s>Svāhā</s> to <s>Govinda</s>, the bearer of the bow, whose radiance is like the moon!

Praise the renowned one, seated in the chariot-seat, the youthful one, terrible as a wild beast, the fierce striker;
be gracious to the worshipper, O <s>Rudra</s> — being praised, may your armies strike another, not us!
May the missile of <s>Rudra</s> turn aside from us; may the evil intent of the fierce, malevolent one [pass us by];
loosen your firm [bow] for the generous ones; O bountiful one, be gracious to our offspring and descendants!
O most bountiful, O most auspicious one, be auspicious to us, be of good mind;
having placed your weapon on the highest tree, clad in a skin, approach — come bearing the <s>Pināka</s> (bow)!
Worthy, you bear arrows and bow; worthy, you bear the golden, adorable ornament of universal form; worthy, you apportion this entire vast [universe] — there is none mightier than you, O <s>Rudra</s>!
You, O <s>Agni</s>, are <s>Rudra</s>, the <s>Asura</s> (lord) of the great heaven; you rule over the <s>Marut</s>-host in nourishment;
you go with the ruddy winds, bringing bliss; you, like <s>Pūṣan</s>, protect the worshipper by your own self.

We know <s>Nārāyaṇa</s>, we meditate on <s>Vāsudeva</s>;
may that <s>Viṣṇu</s> inspire us!
We know the Great Goddess (<s>Mahā-devī</s>), we meditate on the consort of <s>Viṣṇu</s>;
may that <s>Lakṣmī</s> inspire us!
We know the Bow-bearer, we meditate on All Perfection;
may that <s>Dharā</s> (the Earth) inspire us!
We know the friend of <s>Śrī</s> and <s>Bhū</s>, we meditate on the consort of <s>Viṣṇu</s>;
may that <s>Nīlā</s> inspire us!
We know the Overseer of the Worlds, we meditate on the Overseer of the Earth;
may that <s>Kūrma</s> (the Tortoise) inspire us!

We know the Earth-boar (<s>Bhū-varāha</s>), we meditate on <s>Hiraṇyagarbha</s> (the Golden Embryo);
may that <s>Kroḍa</s> (the Boar) inspire us!
We know the <s>Vajra</s>-clawed One, we meditate on the Sharp-tusked One;
may that <s>Narasiṃha</s> (the Man-Lion) inspire us!
We know the Lord of Speech, we meditate on <s>Hayagrīva</s> (the Horse-headed One);
may that <s>Haṃsa</s> (the Swan) inspire us!
We know the one whose limbs are the <s>Vedānta</s>, we meditate on <s>Hayagrīva</s>;
may that <s>Hayagrīva</s> inspire us!
We know the Swan of Swans (<s>Haṃsa-haṃsa</s>), we meditate on the Supreme Swan (<s>Parama-haṃsa</s>);
may that <s>Haṃsa</s> inspire us!
